# SE Lab 2: Agile Backlog Creation & Sprint Simulation in Jira

**Vaibhav .N** · PES University, Dept. of CSE
**System:** Personalized Meal & Diet Subscription Manager (Problem Statement #19, Healthcare & Telemedicine)
**Jira project:** Diet Subscription Manager (`DSM`) — company-managed Scrum project
**Date:** 31 August 2026

---

## 1. Scope and Source Requirements

Lab 1 produced a requirements table for the Personalized Meal & Diet Subscription Manager: a
clinical dietary subscription service that generates weekly menus from patient metabolic
constraints and tracks daily delivery fulfilment. This lab converts that requirements table into
an Agile backlog and runs it through two sprints in Jira.

The handout's worked example uses a Coffee Ordering Kiosk. Its instructions, however, say to
review and reuse *your own* Lab 1 functional requirements, so the backlog below is built from the
Lab 1 requirements table and every story carries a traceability note back to its `FR`/`NFR` ID.

| Lab 1 Req | Description (abridged) | Priority | Where it lands in the backlog |
|---|---|---|---|
| FR-001 | Generate weekly plans within macro constraints and allergen exclusions | High | DSM-6, DSM-7 |
| FR-002 | Create and update a health profile (allergens, calorie goal, carb limit) | High | DSM-4, DSM-5 |
| FR-003 | Dietitian approves/rejects each plan, reason required on rejection | High | DSM-8, DSM-9 |
| FR-004 | Pause/skip/reschedule a delivery; reject inside the 12-hour cut-off | High | DSM-10, DSM-11 |
| FR-005 | Record delivery outcome with timestamp, visible to subscriber and dietitian | Medium | DSM-12 |
| NFR-001 | Schedule modifications performant and authenticated up to the cut-off | High | DSM-11 |
| NFR-002 | Health data encrypted at rest, access-controlled, audited | High | Constraint on DSM-4/DSM-5 |

---

## 2. Epics

Three epics group the requirements into functional themes.

| Key | Epic | What it covers | Requirements |
|---|---|---|---|
| DSM-1 | Health Profile & Meal Plan Generation | A subscriber records their medical dietary constraints and the system builds a compliant weekly menu from them. | FR-001, FR-002, NFR-002 |
| DSM-2 | Dietitian Plan Review & Approval | No meal plan reaches a subscriber until a qualified dietitian has signed it off. | FR-003 |
| DSM-3 | Delivery Scheduling & Fulfilment Tracking | Subscribers change upcoming deliveries within the kitchen's cut-off; the system records what was actually delivered. | FR-004, FR-005, NFR-001 |

---

## 3. User Stories, Story Points and Priority

Nine user stories, all in the *As a / I want / So that* form.

| Key | Epic | User Story | Priority | Points | Traces to |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| DSM-4 | DSM-1 | **Create health profile** — As a subscriber, I want to enter my allergen exclusions, daily calorie goal and carbohydrate limit, so that my meal plans are built around my medical constraints. | High | 5 | FR-002 |
| DSM-6 | DSM-1 | **Generate weekly meal plan** — As a subscriber, I want the system to generate a 7-day menu from my saved profile, so that I do not have to plan meals for the week myself. | High | 8 | FR-001 |
| DSM-7 | DSM-1 | **Block excluded allergens from a plan** — As a subscriber with a food allergy, I want the system to reject any recipe containing an allergen on my exclusion list, so that I am never served something unsafe. | High | 5 | FR-001 |
| DSM-8 | DSM-2 | **View plans awaiting review** — As a dietitian, I want to see a queue of draft plans waiting on me, so that I can work through them before subscribers are left waiting. | High | 3 | FR-003 |
| DSM-9 | DSM-2 | **Approve or reject a plan with a reason** — As a dietitian, I want to approve a draft plan, or reject it with a written reason, so that an unsafe plan is never released to the subscriber. | High | 5 | FR-003 |
| DSM-10 | DSM-3 | **Skip or pause an upcoming delivery** — As a subscriber, I want to skip or pause a delivery that is already scheduled, so that I am not sent food I will not be there to eat. | High | 5 | FR-004 |
| DSM-5 | DSM-1 | **Update health profile** — As a subscriber, I want to edit my allergens and calorie goal at any time, so that my plans stay correct when my diet changes. | Medium | 3 | FR-002 |
| DSM-11 | DSM-3 | **Enforce the 12-hour change cut-off** — As a kitchen manager, I want schedule changes made less than 12 hours before dispatch to be refused, so that we do not waste food that has already been procured. | Medium | 3 | FR-004, NFR-001 |
| DSM-12 | DSM-3 | **Record delivery outcome** — As a delivery agent, I want to mark each delivery as Delivered, Failed or Not Attempted with a timestamp, so that the subscriber and dietitian can see what actually arrived. | Medium | 3 | FR-005 |

**Total backlog estimate: 40 story points across 9 stories.**

### Estimating with planning poker

Story points measure relative effort — complexity, volume of work, and uncertainty — not hours.
The Fibonacci scale (1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13 …) is used deliberately: the gaps widen as the numbers
grow, which forces a decision rather than letting the team argue between a 6 and a 7 on work
nobody understands well enough to separate.

In a team setting each story would be read out, briefly discussed, and every member would reveal
a card at the same time so that nobody anchors on the loudest voice. Where the cards agree the
estimate is taken; where they diverge the two extremes explain their reasoning and the round is
re-run. The point of the discussion is not to converge on a number but to surface the hidden
assumption causing the spread — which is usually where the risk in the story is.

Applying that here, DSM-6 is the only 8 in the backlog. Generating a weekly menu is not one
operation: it is constraint solving over a recipe set, seven days of totals to balance, and a
fallback path when no compliant menu exists. That uncertainty, not raw size, is what pushed it a
full Fibonacci step above the 5s. The estimate is revisited in the reflection below, because
DSM-6 is the story that did not finish.

### Prioritising and ranking the backlog

Priority was set on user impact rather than convenience: a requirement whose failure is a
clinical safety incident outranks one whose failure is an inconvenience. That puts allergen
exclusion (DSM-7) and dietitian approval (DSM-9) at High, and the fulfilment-record and cut-off
stories at Medium.

The backlog was then **reordered** so rank matches priority — all six High stories sit above the
three Medium ones, with dependency order preserved inside each band (a profile must exist before
a plan can be generated from it, and plans must exist before a dietitian can review them).
Both sprints then pull from the top of that ranked list rather than cherry-picking, which is what
makes the ranking meaningful rather than decorative.

One consequence is visible in the plan: DSM-5 *Update health profile* is Medium, so it ranks
below all six High stories and lands in Sprint 2 even though it belongs to the same epic as
DSM-4, which shipped in Sprint 1.

---

## 4. Sprint Execution

Two sprints were run, each with a one-week duration. Both were simulated: the stories were moved
To Do → In Progress → Done inside the Jira board within a single session, so the calendar
duration on the sprint is one week while the recorded activity is compressed into minutes. This
is the simulation the lab asks for, and it is worth naming because it is visible in the shape of
the burndown chart.

### Sprint 1

> **Goal:** A subscriber can set up a dietary profile and get a compliant weekly plan that a
> dietitian can approve or reject.

Sprint 1 took the top five stories off the ranked backlog.

| Key | Story | Points | Outcome |
|---|---|---|---|
| DSM-4 | Create health profile | 5 | Done |
| DSM-6 | Generate weekly meal plan | 8 | **Not completed — carried over** |
| DSM-7 | Block excluded allergens from a plan | 5 | Done |
| DSM-8 | View plans awaiting review | 3 | Done |
| DSM-9 | Approve or reject a plan with a reason | 5 | Done |

**Committed: 26 points. Delivered: 18 points. Carried over: 8 points.**

The sprint goal was only partly met. Profile setup, allergen validation and the whole dietitian
review path shipped — but the plan generation those pieces exist to serve did not, so at the end
of Sprint 1 the dietitian had an empty review queue and there was nothing to approve.

### Sprint 2

> **Goal:** Finish weekly plan generation, let subscribers change an upcoming delivery, and allow
> profile edits to flow into the next plan.

| Key | Story | Points | Outcome |
|---|---|---|---|
| DSM-6 | Generate weekly meal plan (carried over) | 8 | Done |
| DSM-10 | Skip or pause an upcoming delivery | 5 | Done |
| DSM-5 | Update health profile | 3 | Done |

**Committed: 16 points. Delivered: 16 points.**

Sprint 2 was planned smaller than Sprint 1, taking the carry-over first and then continuing down
the ranked backlog. The goal was met in full.

### Progress comments

Progress was recorded as comments on the Jira issues rather than only as status changes, so the
board carries the reasoning and not just the outcome. Short working notes went on each story as
it closed — for example, on DSM-7: *"Allergen exclusion check wired into the plan builder.
Verified against the peanut case called out in FR-001."*

The comment that matters is on DSM-6, written when it became clear it would not finish:

> *"Not finishing this sprint. The generator builds a compliant single day, but balancing a full
> week inside the calorie tolerance still fails on roughly one profile in four, and there is no
> fallback yet for when no compliant menu exists. Carrying over to Sprint 2. In hindsight this
> should have been split into three stories at planning rather than estimated as one 8."*

That note is what makes the carry-over auditable at the retrospective instead of appearing as an
unexplained slip.

### Position at the end of two sprints

| | Points | Stories |
|---|---|---|
| Delivered | 34 | 7 |
| Remaining in backlog | 6 | 2 (DSM-11, DSM-12) |
| **Total** | **40** | **9** |

Velocity: **18 points, then 16 points.**

---

## 5. Reflection

### Did the estimations reflect the actual effort?

Mostly, with one clear miss. The 3s and 5s behaved as estimated — the profile story, the allergen
check and the two dietitian stories were well understood and nothing surprising surfaced while
working them. DSM-6 at 8 points was the exception, and it was under-estimated rather than
mis-sized: the 8 was chosen to signal *"this one is uncertain"*, but the estimate still rested on
an assumption that plan generation could be treated as a single story. It could not.

It should have been split. Generating a candidate menu, balancing daily totals against the
profile, and handling the case where no compliant menu exists are three separable pieces of work,
and splitting them would have let part of the value land inside Sprint 1 instead of the whole
story sliding. That is exactly what happened once it was split in Sprint 2, where it finished
without difficulty.

The lesson worth carrying forward is that a large Fibonacci number is a signal to split a story,
not merely a label for a big one.

### Was the backlog well-prioritised?

Yes, and the outcome supports it. Priority followed user impact, the backlog was ranked to match,
and both sprints pulled from the top of that ranking. The stories whose failure would be a
clinical safety incident — allergen exclusion (DSM-7) and dietitian approval (DSM-9) — were High
and shipped inside the first two sprints. The two stories still outstanding (DSM-11 cut-off
enforcement, DSM-12 delivery-outcome recording) are both Medium. Nothing High is left undone.

One ordering choice is arguable in hindsight. DSM-8, the dietitian's review queue, was pulled into
Sprint 1 alongside the plan generation it depends on. When DSM-6 slipped, DSM-8 shipped into a
system with no plans to review — three points of finished work that delivered no usable value
until the following sprint. Ranking by impact is right; sequencing a dependent story into the
same sprint as the work it depends on is a risk that should have been visible at planning time.

### How did the simulated sprint align with the plan?

Sprint 1 did not align: 26 points committed, 18 delivered, with the single largest story missing
entirely. Sprint 2 aligned exactly — 16 committed, 16 delivered — but partly because it was
planned *after* watching Sprint 1 miss, and was sized more conservatively with the carry-over
taken first.

That is the honest reading: the plan improved because the first sprint's failure informed it,
which is the feedback loop Scrum exists to create. Two sprints is far too small a sample to call
a stable velocity, but 18 and 16 are close enough to suggest a real capacity somewhere near 17
points per sprint, against the 26 initially committed.

### What did the burndown chart show about capacity?

The Sprint 1 chart carries two readings, and the honest one needs both the plot and the event log
beneath it.

The event log is where the burndown is legible. Sprint 1 opened with scope building to 26 points,
then stepped down as each story closed:

| Event | Points burned | Remaining |
|---|---|---|
| Sprint start, all 5 stories added | — | 26 |
| DSM-4 *Create health profile* completed | 5 | 21 |
| DSM-7 *Block excluded allergens* completed | 5 | 16 |
| DSM-8 *View plans awaiting review* completed | 3 | 13 |
| DSM-9 *Approve or reject a plan* completed | 5 | 8 |
| Sprint ended | — | **8 (DSM-6 unfinished)** |

The line stops at 8 and never reaches the axis. That gap *is* the capacity finding: 26 points were
committed and 8 were still open when the time-box expired, so the commitment ran roughly 44% above
what was actually delivered.

The plotted curve itself is less informative, and it is worth saying why rather than pretending
otherwise. The sprint was given the one-week duration the handout asks for, but the To Do →
In Progress → Done moves were all made inside a single lab session, so every event lands in the
first few minutes of a seven-day axis and the remaining-work line renders as a near-vertical drop
at the left edge instead of a staircase descending beside the grey guideline. That is an artifact
of simulating a week's work in one sitting, not a property of the plan.

The instructive part is that a real sprint showing this shape — nothing moving for most of the
time-box, then everything closing at once — would be a genuine warning sign, and for one story it
actually was. DSM-6 was too large to close incrementally, which is precisely the failure the
handout describes as *"a sharp drop during the sprint … work has not been estimated accurately, or
broken down properly."*

Read together with the velocity figures, the practical conclusion is that 26 points was an
over-commitment, and planning the next sprint around 16–18 points would be the defensible forecast.

### What the team would take into the next sprint

Three things, in order of how much they would change the outcome:

1. **Split anything estimated at 8 or above before committing to it.** DSM-6 is the whole argument.
2. **Do not put a story in the same sprint as the work it depends on** unless the dependency is
   scheduled to finish early in that sprint. DSM-8 delivered nothing usable for a week.
3. **Commit to about 17 points, not 26,** until there is enough velocity history to justify more.

